+# Codex execution
+
+Read this reference before spawning, steering, waiting for, or collecting pstack delegates. Resolve every bundled path from the directory containing the actual installed `SKILL.md`, not from the workspace or a guessed installation directory.
+
+## Roles, models, and effort
+
+Run the sibling setup helper with its absolute path:
+
+```text
+node <skills>/setup-pstack/scripts/models.mjs resolve --role "<role>"
+```
+
+It reads `$CODEX_HOME/pstack-for-codex/models.json`, or `~/.codex/pstack-for-codex/models.json` when `CODEX_HOME` is unset. The response contains `role`, ordered `entries`, `source`, and `file`. Each entry supplies `model` and its saved budget's starting `reasoningEffort`. A missing role uses the bundled default. All default models are `gpt-6.1-sol`.
+
+Preserve the ordered entries and their multiplicity. The `arena runners`, `architect runners`, and `interrogate reviewers` defaults each contain two seats. The `arena cross-judge pool` contains two choices, from which the caller selects one judge. Equal entries remain separate executions.
+
+For an explicit model, use the task's explicit effort when given; otherwise adjust the entry's starting effort for the job using poteto-mode's Subagents guidance and the actual host model catalog. Pass model and effort as separate fields. Use only models supported by the native Codex host. A refused model follows the caller's documented fallback, and every fallback is reported. A rejected adjusted effort returns to the role's supported starting effort and is reported. Never invent a model ID or attach effort or speed suffixes.
+
+`auto` and `inherit-parent` both select the current parent model. Budget changes preserve these aliases. Check the active native subagent defaults and selected role before binding them: an omitted model or effort can select `[agents]` defaults, and an omitted agent type can apply the configured `default` role. Omit model and effort only when they will preserve the parent selection. Otherwise pass the observed parent model and effort explicitly through supported fields, using a fresh-context brief when the host requires it. If the parent settings or effective inheritance cannot be established, report the alias as unresolved. An alias is not a refused model and does not use a fallback model.
+
+Speed uses a separate host control when one is exposed. Native subagents inherit the root session's service tier when no per-agent speed control is available. Report an urgent fast request that the host cannot express independently.
+
+Record the actual model and effort for each execution when the host exposes them; otherwise distinguish requested settings from unknown effective settings. Validate required distinct models or families before launching the affected step; report a configuration error when the selected entries cannot satisfy it. A distinct-model or distinct-family requirement remains a requirement. Repeated seats on one model do not satisfy it. Keep a source preference distinct from a requirement and report an unmet preference accurately.
+
+## Native delegation
+
+Inspect the tools exposed by the current runtime and use their actual schemas. Use the native Codex subagent tools, not Cursor `Task` parameters. On a runtime exposing `collaboration`:
+
+- Spawn with `spawn_agent`, a unique `task_name`, and a complete `message`.
+- The message includes the goal, owned files or slice, boundaries, exact verification, required report, and absolute skill/reference/artifact paths. For writers, say they are not alone and must accommodate others' edits.
+- General-purpose workflow seats explicitly select `agent_type: "pstack-general-purpose"`. Setup registers this pstack routing role with a description only, without a config file or instruction body. It preserves the parent-derived instructions and selected model/effort instead of applying the user's configured `default` role. Inspect the actual schema and role precedence. Check effective settings when the host exposes them; otherwise record them as unknown instead of inferring missing response fields.
+- Playbook delegates select the registered `poteto-agent` custom role through `agent_type`. Comment cleanup selects `comment-sicko`. Pass the absolute `poteto-mode/SKILL.md` path to every `poteto-agent`; its preserved instruction body uses that path to find the bundled principles.
+- Resolve custom roles through native discovery after setup. Confirm both the loaded role and a role-selector field in the actual spawn schema. If either is unavailable, inspect registration, enabled config layers and project trust, and the host's tool capabilities; report the specific gap. Use the authorized setup path for missing registration and start a new Codex session before retrying, as setup requires. Preserve the requested custom role's instructions.
+- Explicit `model` and `reasoning_effort` overrides use `fork_turns: "none"` on schemas that require it. Supply all task context in the message. Inherited aliases use the parent-selection binding above; a fresh brief can still use `fork_turns: "none"`.
+
+Spawn independent seats consecutively without waiting between them. Spawning returns an identity while the agent runs asynchronously, so background execution does not require a `run_in_background` argument. A concurrency limit affects how many run at once, not how many seats the workflow requires. Queue remaining seats and drain them; preserve every required result.
+
+## Local worktrees
+
+Workers run on the current Codex host. Give each independent worker an exclusive Git worktree and branch when it writes code. Use native managed-worktree tools when exposed, waiting for creation to complete, or `git worktree add` with an explicit base ref and a local `codex/` branch. The worker's prompt names its absolute checkout, branch, and resolved starting SHA; every shell command runs in that checkout. Keep the coordinator's checkout separate.
+
+For workflows that previously started from the repository's default branch, resolve the actual default ref and SHA before creating the worker checkouts. A caller-supplied non-default ref replaces that base. Measurements and verification pin the exact SHAs required by their brief. Local uncommitted changes enter a worker's input only when the brief explicitly includes them.
+
+The coordinator creates and removes worktrees and performs shared Git operations. Workers own their checkout and output only. Worktrees isolate files and indexes; they share the host, repository metadata, credentials, and other machine resources. Assign distinct ports, temporary/cache/data directories, simulator/device resources, and output locations for work that uses them. Serialize a machine resource that cannot be separated. Restrict the brief's commands to the assigned resources.
+
+After collecting and verifying artifacts, integrate accepted work deliberately. Retain any unintegrated changes and evidence. Remove temporary worktrees and local branches only after their needed work has been integrated or preserved, following the worktree cleanup playbook. A completed worker alone does not authorize discarding its checkout.
+
+## Wait, results, and continuation
+
+Track every returned agent identity and its slice. Use `wait_agent` for an event and `list_agents` for status on runtimes with those tools. The final-answer notification contains the result. Use a result/retrieval tool when the runtime exposes one. A spawn acknowledgement or a completed status without its report is not a result.
+
+Wait for every required terminal result before synthesis. Record failures and missing reports as gaps under the calling workflow's dropout rule. Review changed artifacts and verification evidence yourself.
+
+Fresh agents take new rounds with the consolidated brief. Reuse an existing agent only for the stateful exceptions in poteto-mode. Use `send_message` to steer a running agent, `followup_task` to continue an idle agent, and `interrupt_agent` for a stop/hold on runtimes exposing those controls. Use a native resume control only when present. Keep a stop order separate from a new work brief.
+
+For a repeated watcher or work that must resume after the active turn, read [Codex loops](codex-loops.md) and use its native event or heartbeat controls.
+
+## Permissions and MCP access
+
+Native v2 delegates use the parent's sandbox, approvals, and available tools. Route the role and its normal task procedures through native delegation. MCP-backed `why` and `reflect` delegates use the inherited tools for investigation and citation checks.
+
+## References
+
+[Codex subagents and custom-agent permissions](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/agent-configuration/subagents).
